## 8.  Counting argument for the whole configuration\n\n> \u201cThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n> \\[\n> |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nTriangular number, correct.\n\n> \u201cIf we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (2.5) yields  \n\n> \\[\n> k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nSince each sunny line can cover at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points, the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most the left\u2011hand side. To cover all \\(|T_k|\\) points we need the inequality as written. Correct.\n\n> *Even \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then the left\u2011hand side of (3.2) equals\n> \\(2m\\cdot m=m(2m)=k^{2}/2\\), whereas the right\u2011hand side equals\n> \\(m(2m+1)=k^{2}/2+m\\).  Because \\(m>0\\), (3.2) is false.  Hence **no even\n> \\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**.\n\n**Verification:**  \nAlgebraic manipulation is correct; the inequality fails for any even \\(k\\ge2\\). The conclusion is valid.\n\n> *Odd \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\)\n> and (3.2) becomes an equality:\n\n> \\[\n> (2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 .\n> \\tag{3.3}\n> \\]\n\n> Consequently every sunny line must contain **exactly** \\(m+1\\) points of\n> \\(T_{2m+1}\\), and the \\(k\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.\n\n**Verification:**  \nFor odd \\(k\\) the inequality becomes an equality, forcing each line to attain the bound and the point\u2011sets to be disjoint. Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Sunny lines that attain the bound\n\n> \u201cEquation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:\n\n> * \\(p=q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(1\\));\n> * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(-2\\));\n> * \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\)\u2003(slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\u201d\n\n**Verification (Issue\u202f1):**  \nThe condition \\(|p+q|=2\\) is **not** satisfied by the second and third pairs:\n- For \\((p,q)=(-2,1)\\) we have \\(p+q=-1\\) (absolute value\u202f1).\n- For \\((p,q)=(-1,2)\\) we have \\(p+q=1\\) (absolute value\u202f1).\n\nThus the statement is factually incorrect. The three listed lines are indeed sunny and will be used later, but the justification \u201cthey arise from \\(|p+q|=2\\)\u201d is false. This is a **Justification Gap** (a presentation error that does not affect the final answer).\n\n> \u201cFor \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point\u2011sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.\u201d\n\n**Verification (Issue\u202f2):**  \n- The claim that *only* these three lines can contain \\(m+1\\) points is **not proved**.  \n- The statement \u201c\\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\) gives a *strictly* smaller bound\u201d is false: for \\(q=2\\) the bound \\(\\lfloor k/q\\rfloor+1\\) equals \\(\\lfloor (2m+1)/2\\rfloor+1 = m+1\\), i.e. it **exactly** matches the required number. Hence the argument that the bound is *strictly* smaller for all such lines is incorrect.  \n- Consequently the deduction \u201chence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\)\u201d (i.e. \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=3\\)) rests on an incomplete case analysis. The conclusion is true, but the reasoning is not fully rigorous.\n\nThus this paragraph contains a **Justification Gap** (the logical step that only three families of lines can achieve the bound is insufficiently justified, and the inequality claim for the \\(|p+q|=1\\) case is inaccurate).\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n> **\\(k=0\\).**  \n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and cover\n> every point of \\(S_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nVertical lines cover all points in their columns; the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) together contain exactly the set \\(S_n\\). The lines are distinct and non\u2011sunny. Correct.\n\n> **\\(k=1\\).**  \n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n> The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The sunny line  \n\n> \\[\n> \\ell:\\; y=2x-2n+1\n> \\]\n\n> passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n> Together the \\(n\\) lines satisfy the requirements.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe line has slope\u202f2 (neither 0, \u221e, nor \u20131), so it is sunny. Substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=2n-2n+1=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on it. No other point of \\(S_n\\) is missed because all points with \\(x\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines. The construction is valid.\n\n> **\\(k=3\\).**  \n> \u201cUse the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n> The uncovered set is \\(T_3\\).  The three sunny lines\n\n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\n> L_{2}&: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n> L_{3}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]\n\n> contain respectively the points  \n\n> \\[\n> \\begin{array}{l}\n> L_{1}:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\\\[2pt]\n> L_{2}:\\;(1,3),(2,1),\\\\[2pt]\n> L_{3}:\\;(1,2),(3,1),\n> \\end{array}\n> \\]\n\n> which are exactly the six points of \\(T_3\\).  Hence all points of\n> \\(S_n\\) are covered.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n\\(T_3=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le4\\}\\) consists of the six points listed. Direct substitution shows each point lies on the indicated line, and the three lines are distinct and sunny (slopes 1, \u20132, \u2013\u00bd). The construction covers all points of \\(S_n\\). Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 11.  Conclusion\n\n> \u201cThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\n> lines (with exactly \\(k\\) sunny) covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n> \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) exists are  \n\n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) are valid, and the counting argument together with the (incomplete) case analysis rules out all other \\(k\\). The final answer matches the known solution to the problem.  \n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment**\n\nThe solution reaches the correct set of admissible values \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\). However:\n\n1. The reduction to vertical non\u2011sunny lines (Step\u202f1) is not rigorously justified.\n2. The classification of the lines that attain the bound (Step\u202f4) contains a factual mis\u2011statement and an insufficient argument that no other sunny line can achieve the bound.\n3. The claim that the bound is \u201cstrictly smaller\u201d for \\(|p+q|=1,\\;q\\ge2\\) is inaccurate.\n\nThese issues are **Justification Gaps** (they affect the rigor of the proof but not the correctness of the final answer). No step contains a logical fallacy that makes the final answer false, so the solution is **not fully rigorous** but **not invalid**."

